# kJubilee — station health watch
#
# Install on the app host:
#   cp scripts/kjubilee-health.cron /etc/cron.d/kjubilee-health
#   chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/kjubilee-health
#   systemctl restart cron
#
# ── why this exists, and why the nightly publish was not enough ──────────────
# kjubilee-schedules.cron publishes a week of day files once a day. That covers
# the failure where programming runs out. It does NOT cover:
#
#   · a day file that publishes EMPTY, or with entries whose audio is not there
#   · a day file that is valid and fully playable but only covers half the day
#   · a day file deleted, expired or overwritten between nightly runs
#   · a station added to the dial after the last nightly run
#   · anything that breaks at 12:05 and would sit broken for 24 hours
#
# All of those look identical to a listener: they press play and hear nothing.
# None of them raise anything anywhere. The only detector this network had was
# somebody tuning in and telling us — which is exactly how HM 361.90 was found.
#
# So: every fifteen minutes, check that every on-air station has a populated day
# file AND that a random sample of the tracks it schedules actually serves. Any
# station missing programming — or carrying a day that stops early or has a hole
# in it — is REPUBLISHED on the spot from the pool this host already holds.
# Missing audio is reported and not guessed at: it needs the music share and a
# decision about what belongs on air, and a script that guessed would be worse
# than one that reports.
#
# THE ALARM IS DRILLED, NOT ASSUMED. tools/drill-station-health.js breaks a
# station three ways behind a proxy — schedule gone, day half-published, audio
# all 404 — and asserts the watch catches each one, with a control run to prove
# the drill itself is not what is failing. Run it after touching the checker.
#
# Fifteen minutes is the interval a listener would forgive. It also keeps the
# sampling honest: six tracks per station per run is ~96 draws a day per
# station, so a systematic audio failure surfaces within a run or two rather
# than eventually.
#
# THE LOG IS THE ALARM. Every run appends; a healthy run is one line. Grep it:
#   grep FAIL /var/log/kjubilee-health.log | tail
#
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

*/15 * * * * root cd /var/www/kjubilee.com && /usr/bin/node tools/check-station-health.js --days 3 --sample 6 --repair --quiet >> /var/log/kjubilee-health.log 2>&1
